description:
Route every CLI test that plants, reads or corrupts lease storage through one test-support module, so the lease store layout is spelled once in the test tree.

## Background

CLI tests reach the claims store directly:

- `tests/cli_item_validate/validation_lease_tests.rs` (`write_claims_index`, a malformed index);
- `tests/cli_repo_workflow/claim_integration_tests.rs` (reads a lease from the index, removes the index's permissions, removes the index in recovery tests);
- `tests/cli_repo_workflow/external_data_root_tests.rs` (asserts `jit/claims.jsonl` exists under a data root outside every checkout);
- `tests/cli_issue/failure_probe_fixture.rs` (`malformed-claim-index`), with setup notes in `tests/cli_issue/failure_lever_registry.toml`;
- `tests/cli_issue/command_exit_code_projection_tests.rs` (corrupts the index).

Shared CLI test code lives in the `test-support` surface: `src/test_cli.rs`, `src/test_utils.rs`, `src/test_harness.rs`, declared in `src/lib.rs` under `#[cfg(any(test, feature = "test-support"))]` (`dev/TESTING.md`). Lease storage lives under `WorktreePaths::shared_jit`, which is `<common-dir>/jit` in primary, linked and external-data-root layouts, so a fixture cannot derive it as `root/.git/jit`.

## Success Criteria

- [hard] REQ-01: One `test-support` module takes `shared_jit` as input and plants a lease, reads a lease, names an issue's lease storage path and corrupts lease storage. The listed tests use it, and no CLI test names `claims.index.json` or `claims.jsonl` outside it.
- [hard] REQ-02: The listed tests pass unchanged in what they assert.

Make every event-appending mutation capture the event log as its bounded end, so no mutation reads the log's records.

## Background

The append base of an event-appending mutation is the log's length and final line (`CaptureSpec::discover_tail`, `crates/jit/src/repository_state/image.rs`). Several captures still request `VirtualPath::EVENTS` whole, reading the full log on every run:

- `publish_captured_issue_mutation` (`commands/mod.rs`), shared by issue lifecycle and field mutations;
- document add, remove and asset rescan (`commands/document.rs`);
- gate evaluation (`capture_gate_evaluation_image`, `commands/gate_check.rs`);
- gate preset apply and gate registry mutations (`commands/gate.rs`);
- template apply (`commands/template.rs`);
- profile apply and refresh (`commands/profile.rs`);
- init and re-initialization over an existing data root (`commands/init.rs`, `repository_state/initialize.rs`, read again by `events_action`, which errors on a tail).

Profile and init reach `capture_proposed_base` with the log among their extra paths (`scaffold.delta_paths()` in `commands/init.rs`, `events_path` in `commands/profile.rs`), and a whole request wins over the base's tail in `discover_tail`, so those extra paths must change too. Their `validate_repository` calls read no event log.

A profile's composition reads only the log's final line (`captured_log_end`), so the torn-tail marker does not hold this back.

## Success Criteria

- [hard] REQ-01: Each listed capture requests the event log through `discover_tail`, and re-initialization decides whether the log exists from the tail entry.
- [hard] REQ-02: A test shows that a mutation from each listed command family succeeds when every line of the event log except the final one is unparseable.

Update the claim command reference, the coordination and troubleshooting guides, and the contributor testing guide for issue-addressed lease commands and per-issue lease records.

## Background

Docs describe lease-id-addressed commands and an index rebuilt from the log:

- `docs/reference/claim.md` (lease-id arguments; the claims log as audit trail; staleness, the `jit claim status` stale marking and its heartbeat remedy);
- `docs/reference/cli-command-grammar.md` (renew, heartbeat and force-evict take the lease's own id);
- `docs/how-to/multi-agent-coordination.md` ("Rebuild claims index from log");
- `docs/how-to/dependency-management.md` ("rebuildable index");
- `docs/concepts/guarantees.md` (the lease-id commands in its eviction and heartbeat examples);
- `docs/tutorials/parallel-work-worktrees.md`;
- `docs/how-to/troubleshooting.md` (rebuild the index; delete `claims.jsonl` and the index by hand);
- `docs/reference/worktree-validate.md` and `docs/reference/cli-commands.md` (claims-index validation, recover rebuilding the index);
- `dev/TESTING.md` (rebuild property tests and claims-index validation);
- `.agents/skills/jit-manage/SKILL.md` (the lease record lives in `claims.index.json`).

After this epic: renew, heartbeat and force-evict take an issue id, and force-evict also reaches a lease whose issue is deleted; leases are `.git/jit/leases/<issue-id>.json`; `claim list` and `claim status` report unexpired leases and mark a stale one stale; recover cleans locks, evicts expired leases and reclaims stale indefinite leases; validation does not read the claims files. `scripts/hooks/README.md` already says no hook checks leases and needs no change.

## Success Criteria

- [hard] REQ-01: Every listed page addresses renew, heartbeat and force-evict by issue, and none tells an operator to rebuild, delete or edit an index or the claims log.
- [hard] REQ-02: The recover and validation descriptions match their behaviour, and `claim.md` states that listings mark a stale lease stale and that a heartbeat refreshes it or recover reclaims it.
- [hard] REQ-03: `dev/TESTING.md` names the claim property tests and coordinator tests that exist.
